{"name":"Malachite Green","hex":"#004e00","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"malachite green","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A toxic chemical used as a dye, as a treatment for infections in fish (when diluted), and as a bacteriological stain"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/malachite_green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Malachite green","blocks":[{"text":"Malachite green is an organic compound that is used as a dyestuff and controversially as an antimicrobial in aquaculture. Malachite green is traditionally used as a dye for materials such as silk, leather, and paper. Despite its name the dye is not prepared from the mineral malachite; the name just comes from the similarity of color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Malachite_green_structure.svg/330px-Malachite_green_structure.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malachite_green","wikidata":"Q408815"},{"title":"Malachite","blocks":[{"text":"Malachite is a copper carbonate hydroxide mineral, with the formula Cu2CO3(OH)2. This opaque, green-banded mineral crystallizes in the monoclinic crystal system, and most often forms botryoidal, fibrous, or stalagmitic masses, in fractures and deep, underground spaces, where the water table and hydrothermal fluids provide the means for chemical precipitation. Individual crystals are rare, but occur as slender to acicular prisms. Pseudomorphs after more tabular or blocky azurite crystals also occur."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Malachite%2C_Zaire.jpg/330px-Malachite%2C_Zaire.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malachite","wikidata":"Q164411"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"},{"title":"Shades of green","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color green may differ in hue, chroma or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a green or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Shades_of_Green.svg/330px-Shades_of_Green.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_green","wikidata":"Q19847180"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Malachite_green_oxalate.jpg/500px-Malachite_green_oxalate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Malachite_green_oxalate.jpg/500px-Malachite_green_oxalate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMalachite_green_oxalate.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/MalachiteGr%26Leuco.png/500px-MalachiteGr%26Leuco.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/MalachiteGr%26Leuco.png/1280px-MalachiteGr%26Leuco.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/MalachiteGr%26Leuco.png/500px-MalachiteGr%26Leuco.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"On the left is leuco-malachite Green (LMG) and on the right are the two equivalent resonance structures of the MG cation. The alcohol derivative of MG is derived from LMG by replacement of the unique C–H by C–OH.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMalachiteGr%26Leuco.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Malachite_green_preparation1.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Malachite_green_preparation1.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Malachite_green_preparation1.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-Malachite_green_preparation1.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Malachite_green_preparation1.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Malachite_green_preparation1.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Synthesis of malachite green","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMalachite_green_preparation1.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Bacillus_subtilis_Spore.jpg/330px-Bacillus_subtilis_Spore.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=pars